# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site data. NEVER COMMIT ANY OF THIS.
# Captures, exports and sitemaps contain real MAC addresses, serial numbers and
# the physical layout of the site. Sanitized samples are the only exception,
# and they must be named sample-*.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
captures/
exports/
sitemaps/
*.jsonl
*.pcap
*.pcapng

*.csv
*.tsv
*.xlsx
!**/sample-*.csv
!**/sample-*.tsv

# Session state written during a walk
sessions/
*.session.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leading slash matters here too: a bare "dist/" matches at any depth and
# would silently swallow gui/frontend/dist, which is the application's UI and
# has to be committed for the build to embed it.
/dist/
# Only the compiled output. gui/build also holds the app icon and the platform
# manifests, which have to be committed or CI builds with Wails' defaults —
# which is how this shipped with the stock Wails logo for several releases.
/build/
gui/build/bin/
# Compiled binaries left in the working tree by a local `go build`.
#
# Leading slash matters: a bare "capture-tool" would also match the
# cmd/capture-tool source directory and silently drop it from the repo.
#
# The old names stay listed. Renaming the project once left a stale binary
# from the previous name un-ignored, and the next `git add -A` committed
# 2.5MB of it. Cheap insurance against doing that again.
/capture-tool
/capture-tool.exe
/capture-tool-*
/OpenIPReporter
/OpenIPReporter.exe
/OpenIPReporter-*
/ipreporter
/ipreporter.exe
/ipreporter-*
/BetterIPReporter-*
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Go
*.test
*.out
vendor/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors / OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Stray binaries from "go build ./..." inside the app module.
/gui/gui
/gui/gui.exe
